Aim: To automate the deployment, scaling, and management of the containerized User Registration application using Kubernetes.

Requirements:
- Docker
- Kubernetes
- kubectl
- Java Spring Boot application
- Docker image from Exercise 7
Step 1: Build Docker image
mvn clean package


docker build -t user-registration:latest .


Step 2: Create Kubernetes configuration
Create a file named k8s-deployment.yaml.
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment

metadata:
  name: registration-app

spec:
  replicas: 3

  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: registration

  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: registration

    spec:
      containers:
        - name: registration
          image: user-registration:latest
          imagePullPolicy: IfNotPresent

          ports:
            - containerPort: 8080


Step 3: Create Service
Create k8s-service.yaml.
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service

metadata:
  name: registration-service

spec:
  type: NodePort

  selector:
    app: registration

  ports:
    - port: 8080
      targetPort: 8080
      nodePort: 30080


Step 4: Automate deployment
Execute:
kubectl apply -f k8s-deployment.yaml
kubectl apply -f k8s-service.yaml


Output:
deployment.apps/registration-app created
service/registration-service created


Step 5: Verify deployment
kubectl get deployments


kubectl get pods


kubectl get services


Expected output:
NAME              READY   UP-TO-DATE   AVAILABLE
registration-app  3/3     3            3


Step 6: Scale the application
Increase replicas to 5:
kubectl scale deployment registration-app --replicas=5


Check:
kubectl get pods


Output:
5 pods running


Step 7: Update the application
kubectl set image deployment/registration-app registration=user-registration:v2


Check rollout status:
kubectl rollout status deployment/registration-app


For this update command, the user-registration:v2 image must already exist in the cluster's image store or registry. Otherwise, Kubernetes will report an image-pull error.
Step 8: Rollback deployment
kubectl rollout undo deployment/registration-app


Step 9: Delete resources
kubectl delete -f k8s-service.yaml
kubectl delete -f k8s-deployment.yaml


Important Kubernetes commands
Command	Purpose
kubectl get pods	List pods
kubectl get deployments	List deployments
kubectl get services	List services
kubectl apply -f file.yaml	Apply configuration
kubectl delete -f file.yaml	Delete resources
kubectl scale	Scale application
kubectl describe pod	View pod details
kubectl logs pod-name	View logs
kubectl rollout status	Check deployment status
kubectl rollout undo	Roll back deployment

Result: Successfully automated the deployment and management of a containerized Java application using Kubernetes, including scaling and rolling back.
